Chapter 2 of Pippin Barr’s “The Stuff Games Are Made Of” focuses on rules and their manipulation. 
He uses the classic game Pong as an example to illustrate his point that a game is just code, and code can be changed, therefore the rules of the game can change to make them completely unrecognizable. 
I find it to be a great creative exercise. 
Taking something as simple as Pong and trying to turn it into as many different games as possible could lead to some great ideas that can be put to use is other projects. 
Unfair Pong makes me think of a game where the player is constantly at a disadvantage when facing all their opponents. 
That line of thinking could lead to the creation of a game like Dark Souls, where one hit from an enemy could instantly kill you, but you’d need to hit your enemy tens of dozens of times to be able to defeat them.
Pippin really emphasizes the importance of rules and experimenting with them. 
Monopoly without a rulebook, as Barr puts it, is just a pile of cardboard, paper, and plastic, but being able to add or change the rules of the game shows you how malleable a game can be, and how different it can change up the way the game is played and viewed.
Barr also touches on how mistakes can lead to fresh new features for a game. 
In Fertility Pong, a bug in Pippin’s code allows hundreds of balls to spawn in if one ball hits the edge of a paddle at just the right spot, creating a “Pong Armageddon”. 
Many ideas spawn from mistakes. Bob Ross believes that there are no mistakes, just happy little accidents. He incorporates those accidents into his art, like how Pippin incorporates them into his Pong versions. Some games are fun because they’re glitchy, buggy, have exploits. 
They make a game unique!
